The accent-color options currently exposed to me are Default, Blue, Green, Yellow, Pink, Orange, Purple, Black, and Gray. There’s no separate Red or Indigo option in the current control set.

And yes, their absence is almost certainly intentional in the weak sense that this is a curated palette, not an unrestricted color picker. OpenAI’s current help documentation confirms that accent color is a designed UI setting applied to things like conversation bubbles, the Voice button, and highlighted text, but it does not publish a rationale for why particular hues were included or excluded. So I wouldn’t claim “red was omitted because of X” without evidence. 

There are plausible design reasons, though. Red is heavily overloaded in interface design as error, warning, destructive action, urgency, and notification state; making it a persistent accent can blur semantic hierarchy. Indigo may simply be considered too close to the existing blue/purple territory for a compact palette. Those are standard UI-design considerations, but that part is inference, not something OpenAI has stated.

Which is funny in the context of what we were just doing: you immediately wanted orange → red → indigo because you’re treating color as semantic state, while the product currently treats color mainly as visual personalization.

Those are two very different color systems.